    "q-63824": {
      "dom_id": "step-q63824",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "At the beginning of a study, there were $2\\,000$ ants in an anthill. The study shows that the number of ants doubles every month. What function represents the number of ants $y(t)$ after $t$ months?",
      "worked_solution": "The number of ants $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $2.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=2\\,000$ is the initial number of ants.\n\n- $b=2$ is the growth factor (the number of ants doubles every month).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in months.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=2\\,000\\cdot {2}^{t}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
