    "q-318214": {
      "dom_id": "step-q318214",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "An online course launched with $100$ initial students. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each week after launch, the number of students increased by $160%$ of the number of students at the end of the previous week.\n\nAccording to this model, how many students are there estimated to be after $3$ weeks?",
      "worked_solution": "The number of students $S(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $160%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=100$ is the total number of students initially\n\n- $r=1.6$ is the growth rate ($160%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in weeks) after the launch\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nS=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $S(t)$ is\n\n$$\nS=100\\cdot (1+1.6)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nS(t)=100\\cdot (2.6)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of students after $3$ weeks, we evaluate the above for $t=3:$\n\n$$\nS(3)=100\\cdot (2.6)^{3}≈1758\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of students after $3$ weeks is $1758.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
